---
// "How Genshin damage is actually calculated" — the exact seven-factor model
// this calculator runs (KQM general damage formula). Rendered statically so
// every word is crawlable, and kept scannable with a single accordion.
---

<section id="formula" class="page-shell section-pad scroll-mt-20 border-t border-[var(--line)]">
  <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-[var(--text)]">How Genshin damage is actually calculated</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-[var(--muted)]">
    Every hit — normal, skill, burst — is a single product of six factors. This is the exact engine the calculator
    above runs, following the official damage formula as documented by the
    <a href="https://keqingmains.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-forest-600 underline decoration-forest-500/50 underline-offset-2 hover:text-forest-700">KeqingMains theorycrafting library</a>.
    Understand each factor and you can see why a number is what it is — and which single upgrade moves it most.
  </p>

  <div class="panel mt-8 overflow-x-auto p-6">
    <p class="tnum whitespace-nowrap text-center text-sm leading-loose text-[var(--muted)] md:text-base">
      (<span class="text-forest-600">Scaling&nbsp;stat</span> × <span class="text-forest-600">Talent&nbsp;%</span>)
      <span class="mx-2">×</span> (1 + <span class="text-hydro">DMG&nbsp;Bonus</span>)
      <span class="mx-2">×</span> <span class="text-cryo">CRIT</span>
      <span class="mx-2">×</span> <span class="text-pyro">Reaction</span>
      <span class="mx-2">×</span> <span class="text-dendro">DEF</span>
      <span class="mx-2">×</span> <span class="text-electro">RES</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <details class="accordion mt-8">
    <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--surface)] p-5 font-semibold text-[var(--text)] transition-colors hover:border-forest-300">
      <span>Every factor, in detail</span>
      <svg class="h-5 w-5 shrink-0 text-[var(--muted)]" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M12 5v14M5 12h14" /></svg>
    </summary>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">

    <!-- 1. Base damage / scaling -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">1. Base damage <span class="text-forest-600">— the stat that scales</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        A talent does not always scale off ATK. The base is <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">Scaling&nbsp;stat × Talent&nbsp;%</strong>,
        where the scaling stat is <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">ATK, DEF, Max HP or EM</strong> depending on the character —
        Hu&nbsp;Tao, Yelan and Neuvillette scale off HP; Itto, Noelle and Albedo off DEF. This is why "just stack ATK" is
        wrong for roughly half the roster.
      </p>
      <p class="tnum mt-3 rounded-[10px] bg-[var(--surface-2)] p-4 text-center text-sm text-[var(--text)]">
        Total ATK = (Character base + Weapon base) × (1 + ATK%) + Flat ATK
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        The two <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">base</strong> values are the "white" numbers you see on the character and
        weapon. <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">ATK% only multiplies those</strong> — never the flat ATK from the feather or
        Bennett. That is why Bennett's buff scales off base ATK, and why low-base-ATK carries (Hu&nbsp;Tao: 106) get almost
        nothing from an ATK% sands.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 2. Talent multiplier -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">2. Talent multiplier <span class="text-forest-600">— your talent level</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Every talent level is a guaranteed <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">~6–7% increase</strong> on that skill, with no
        RNG and no resin lottery. Going from talent 8 to 10 is often worth more than weeks of artifact farming. The
        calculator uses the <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">real talent-10 multipliers</strong> for each character's
        normal, charged, skill and burst.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 3. DMG bonus -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">3. DMG Bonus <span class="text-hydro">— the additive multiplier</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Goblet, weapon passives, set bonuses and supports (Kazuha, Furina, Yelan) all add into <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">one
        single multiplier</strong>: (1 + total DMG Bonus). Because they stack additively, the more you already have, the less each
        new source is worth — this is exactly why an ATK% goblet can beat an elemental goblet once a character's DMG Bonus is past ~150%.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 4. CRIT -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">4. CRIT <span class="text-cryo">— the 1:2 balance</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        A crit multiplies the hit by (1 + CRIT DMG). Averaged over many hits, expected damage uses the multiplier
        <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">(1 + CRIT Rate × CRIT DMG)</strong>. Because a CRIT Rate roll is worth exactly half a
        CRIT DMG roll, the optimal balance is <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">1:2</strong>: 70%/140% (×1.98) beats
        50%/200% (×2.00 on paper but ×1.86 expected). Aim for 60%+ CRIT Rate on a carry before stacking more CRIT DMG.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 5. Reaction -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">5. Reaction <span class="text-pyro">— three families</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">Amplifying</strong> (Vaporize ×1.5/×2.0, Melt ×2.0/×1.5, depending on which element
        triggers) multiply the whole hit and scale with EM through
        <span class="tnum text-[var(--text)]">1 + 2.78 × EM ÷ (EM + 1400)</span>. They keep CRIT, ATK and level.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">Transformative</strong> (Overload, Electro-Charged, Superconduct, Swirl, Bloom,
        Hyperbloom, Burgeon, Burning) are separate instances that <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">ignore ATK and CRIT</strong>
        — they scale only with character level and EM:
        <span class="tnum text-[var(--text)]">ReactionMult × LevelMult × (1 + 16 × EM ÷ (EM + 2000))</span>. That is why a
        Hyperbloom trigger wants pure EM, never a CRIT circlet.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">Additive</strong> (Aggravate 1.15, Spread 1.25) is added as flat damage to the base
        <em>before</em> DMG Bonus and CRIT, so it benefits from both — Electro + Dendro teams build EM alongside CRIT.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 6. DEF -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">6. DEF multiplier <span class="text-dendro">— why level matters</span></h3>
      <p class="tnum mt-3 rounded-[10px] bg-[var(--surface-2)] p-4 text-center text-sm text-[var(--text)]">
        (Lv + 100) ÷ ((Lv + 100) + (Enemy Lv + 100) × (1 − DEF reduction) × (1 − DEF ignore))
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Against a same-level enemy this is exactly <span class="tnum text-[var(--text)]">0.5</span> — you deal half your
        pre-defense damage. A level-80 carry against a level-90 enemy loses a few percent, which is why levelling a reaction
        carry to 90 matters more than it looks. DEF shred and ignore (Lisa, Raiden C2) are among the rarest, strongest buffs.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- 7. RES -->
    <div class="panel p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--text)]">7. RES multiplier <span class="text-electro">— shred beats stats</span></h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Resistance = enemy RES − shred:
        <span class="tnum text-[var(--text)]">below 0 → 1 − RES/2, 0–75% → 1 − RES, 75%+ → 1 ÷ (1 + 4 × RES)</span>.
        Most enemies sit at 10%, so the baseline multiplier is 0.9. The Viridescent Venerer 4-piece shreds 40% RES, flipping a
        10%-RES enemy to −30% — a <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">~28% damage increase</strong> from a single support, more
        than almost any artifact upgrade.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel border-forest-500/30 bg-forest-500/8 p-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-forest-600">Why it matters: multiplying, not adding</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--muted)]">
        Because all six factors <em>multiply</em>, raising your weakest factor is worth far more than pushing an already-high one
        higher. If your CRIT multiplier is 1.3 while DMG Bonus is already 1.8, one more CRIT roll beats another DMG bonus every
        time. The calculator's highest-value-upgrades list re-simulates each change and ranks them by exactly this logic — start
        with whatever it puts first.
      </p>
    </div>

    </div>
  </details>
</section>
